NVIDIA DLSS Super Resolution of the 3D scene on the rasterizer-ordered view path: d3d12_dlss_mode (off, dlaa, quality,
balanced, performance, ultra_performance), d3d12_dlss_preset, d3d12_dlss_bridge and d3d12_dlss_data_path. The scene
surface is the multisampled 8888 color and depth surface with the most depth-tested draws in a frame. Its draws get a
host viewport and scissor scaled to DLSS's render size about the EDRAM origin (render widths snapped so the EDRAM tile
edges where predicated tiles meet are whole render pixels) and a Halton(2,3) jitter in the NDC offset.
At each resolve of the scene surface, scene_capture copies the resolved region from the EDRAM into render-size
textures: color (samples averaged) and depth (sample 0), each at the first resolve after it was drawn, before a resolve
clears it. Right after the game's own resolve, which holds the smaller scene in a corner, scene_encode stretches the
region to full size into a buffer laid out like a 1x EDRAM, and D3D12RenderTargetCache::CopyResolveToMemory, the copying
half of Resolve split out, copies it into the game's texture, so draws reading it before the scene is done see it whole.
At the first draw outside the scene, or the swap, scene_motion makes camera motion vectors from the depth and a
clip-to-previous-clip matrix, DLSS is evaluated on the command list through DeferredCommandList::ExternalCall, and its
output goes the same way into the textures of the color resolves that no later resolve of the frame covers again.
Everything drawn from them afterwards (post-processing, HUD, menus) keeps the full resolution.
The camera matrix comes from draws whose vertex shader writes the position as one constant matrix times a fetched
position (scene_motion_analysis.h reads that from the disassembly), matched across frames by shader, vertex buffer,
indices and occurrence: the previous matrix times the inverse of the current one, taking the one most of them agree on.
Objects that move on their own have no motion vectors of their own. scene_layout.h holds the geometry and math that
need no GPU.
A failure logs why and draws the scene at full resolution for the rest of the session; a scene DLSS can't evaluate in a
frame keeps the stretched copies. Builds on 0160.

